<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cap-Haïtien</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cap-Français, Cap-Henri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cap-Haïtien is a port on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. Founded by the French as Cap-Français, it was the wealthy capital of the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> until 1770 and was burned in 1793 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a> was crowned there as King Henri I in 1811, when it was renamed Cap-Henri; his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle Laferrière</a> and Sans-Souci palace stand nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cap-haitien.jpg" alt="Cap-Haïtien" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rémi Kaupp · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cap-Haitien_seen_from_Morne_Jean.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Spanish explorers took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> name Guárico for the area. Puerto Real, one of the first Spanish towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, was founded near Cap-Haïtien in 1503 and abandoned in 1578. Cap-Français was founded in 1670 by a dozen colonist-adventurers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-dogeron">Bertrand d'Ogeron</a>, and its formal foundation dates from 1711. The French built large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations on the northern plains and imported tens of thousands of African slaves. Cap-Français became the colony's main commercial centre and its capital until 1770, when the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>, and its wealth earned it the nickname <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of the Antilles. Two thirds of its 15,000 inhabitants in 1790 were enslaved.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> began nearby. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bois-caiman">Bois Caïman</a>, on 14 August 1791, the maroon leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutty-boukman">Dutty Boukman</a> and a mambo named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecile-fatiman">Cécile Fatiman</a> led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a> ceremony at which those present swore to die rather than endure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the insurrection began on 22–23 August. At Vertières, on 18 November 1803, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a>'s army defeated a French army under the Comte de Rochambeau, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> declared independence in 1804. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a> renamed the city, and it was the first capital of his Kingdom of Haiti until 1820.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cap-Haïtien is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Nord, with about 300,000 people. Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milot">Milot</a> stand the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle Laferrière</a>, a mountaintop fortress built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a>, and the ruins of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sans-souci-palace">Sans-Souci Palace</a>, damaged in the earthquake of 7 May 1842, which destroyed most of the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed both as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1982. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> from 1915 to 1934, the streets of the central city were renamed with letters running north to south and numbers running east to west. Its well-preserved 19th-century architecture, with gingerbread houses, resembles that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cap-Haïtien” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An earthquake in May 1842 destroyed <b>Cap-Haïtien</b> and other northern towns, and the following year the Reform movement led by Charles Rivière-Hérard rose in the south; Boyer resigned and left for exile in March 1843.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cap-Français</b>, later Cap-Haïtien, was established shortly before 1670, when a tobacco crisis emptied many places and freebooting revived in raids such as Vera Cruz in 1683 and Campêche in 1686.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the top of colonial society stood the grands blancs, the “big whites”: the owners of the great sugar estates, the leading merchants of <b>Cap-Français</b> and Port-au-Prince, and the senior officials and officers.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li><li><blockquote>He was crowned Henri I at <b>Cap-Haïtien</b>, renamed Cap-Henri, in June 1811, while Alexandre Pétion's Republic of Haiti continued to rule the south and west.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-haiti">Kingdom of Haiti</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1807 he set up his own State of Haiti, with its capital at <b>Cap-Haïtien</b> and himself as president for life.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-haiti">State of Haiti</a></li></ul></section>
